o pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). This means $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n![](images/e6/e690911497bbee717323b680e92c57a7ab9d6d9d78d6ed3440d2657cd5883c3d.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we obtain the vector \n![](images/53/53cd895629e3c850067d8e443c5f7ce411d1a9358a6123bc72465fdce673f14c.png) \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{3}.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf x$ as follows:\n\n![](images/86/86d8735a9101ad90efe6c83e21ba6849f488baf3202ff549e9aea92764019c0e.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](images/24/24cd2c6c9a445179df93ecf534fbab1db3906a6db78473505ea0fd6c7de2e466.png)" 13194139534696 17592186045417 false] [17592186133658 1071 "Determine whether the following set is orthogonal:\n![](images/5b/5b51a8ec772d084cb7c4ac85b6e6ac71e9d128242a6553348e7ea9ec57e8c3dc.svg)" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186133658 1071 "Determine whether the following set is orthogonal:\n![](images/8f/8f316d4e8c33c1d8d087f2fb42e283169919a4cbc9c14a67792df70a447a41b9.png)" 13194139534696 17592186045417 false] [17592186133658 1074 "If we denote the vectors in our set ${X}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{x}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${X}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0=2\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo ${X}$ is *not* an orthogonal set." 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186133658 1074 "If we denote the vectors in our set ${X}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{x}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${X}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0=2\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo ${X}$ is *not* an orthogonal set." 13194139534696 17592186045417 false] [17592186133781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433153 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186133782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433149 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186133837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433151 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186133843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433150 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186133843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433152 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186133990 1050 1140.0 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186135045 1064 "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n![](images/9b/9b5afbbe850cb936b16b177e0a1b42bdd94df9bdc5dd3830aadc0de328a2b0d8.svg)\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors." 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186135045 1064 "A set of vectors ${U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is an **orthogonal set** if each pair of distinct vectors from the set is orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following set:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${W}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{w}_1, \\mathbf{w}_2, \\mathbf{w}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${W}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 7=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${W}$ is an orthogonal set.\n\nIf an orthogonal set is also a basis, then we call it an **orthogonal basis**. Here, $W$ is an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ because it contains three linearly independent (you can check this) orthogonal vectors." 13194139534696 17592186045417 false] [17592186351223 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}$" 13194139534696 17592186045417 false] [17592186351223 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$"