gin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=0+10=10\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=12+3-1=14\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"." 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433145 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433151 1069 "q-83768" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433151 1071 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433151 1072 #uuid "9e056f00-625c-54f9-921d-feb61293e496" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433151 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{21}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{14}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}|+|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}|=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{14}}.$" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433146 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433152 1069 "q-140479" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433152 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433152 1072 #uuid "9f013658-0e98-56e3-b0df-e2d26e152fca" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433152 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-3+0-2+2=-3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=4-4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2+0-4+2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+6-4-2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2+2+4=8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ only\"." 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433147 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433153 1069 "q-82399" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433153 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nI. $U=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\}$" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433153 1072 #uuid "bccef2e2-efdc-5751-b184-d287795e3e5f" 13194139534696 17592186045417 true] [17592186433153 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- $\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=0+12+6=18\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{12}{25}-\\frac{12}{25}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{9}{25}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." 13194139534696 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-15b18abf-20d4-5b53-b9f6-8b33f580740b" 17592186433119 "answer-1973b4e5-3021-5f0e-84fb-21f789bd603c" 17592186433120 "answer-2454dddf-8213-5a02-af2b-3a48c11de7c7" 17592186433121 "answer-25cb96ba-aaf0-5535-b2bf-14442cb3e2c8" 17592186433122 "answer-2e486175-d93e-59a1-beee-b5e17fb3fe9f" 17592186433123 "answer-342bd711-7b57-5e5b-b53c-c41f6cf6ed52" 17592186433124 "answer-44ed5e4f-8fa9-5c51-90b0-836168dfa8a9" 17592186433125 "answer-5594b522-b05d-56b3-b912-961b140aeac9" 17592186433126 "answer-625d32a2-e015-5a3c-a155-566556b8fd99" 17592186433127 "answer-7d1dfe6a-46a2-5b7b-b9a2-2e4f0b6a6290" 17592186433128 "answer-8e2ec803-638b-5ea2-ac14-1aac19cbda75" 17592186433129 "answer-8ea03761-fa6e-5cb3-aed6-0f335e32102b" 17592186433130 "answer-8f442901-c3b7-5866-82d6-1cf54562946a" 17592186433131 "answer-902457a9-b694-5355-8804-d4836495776f" 17592186433132 "answer-93c0c141-6245-5b52-b5d1-782e45040c14" 17592186433133 "answer-99a6a3de-f047-5ffa-a17f-a1fea25b4278" 17592186433134 "answer-a054e702-a15a-5f24-9814-706df32db073" 17592186433135 "answer-a2e77911-f7b1-5d0a-99ba-fbc6c3057279" 17592186433136 "answer-ab5a79ce-b244-589b-887d-eb3fa120294b" 17592186433137 "answer-b440af9e-0d7a-5e18-bcec-b34eee928e3f" 17592186433138 "answer-b4b5b8f0-d7c4-56e8-91b3-1eb6a0e8b98a" 17592186433139 "answer-c083b72f-3913-5a9b-bb4b-2b33c44b8374" 17592186433140 "answer-c2644191-8b0e-5649-af0f-0f586b12d76d" 17592186433141 "answer-c833776b-5d21-59ee-8ccb-29989a9b9e8a" 17592186433142 "answer-f906b041-d06e-5690-ab61-f1fbb040be89" 17592186433143 "answer-field-88d26e7d-7cf7-53de-a739-9d6548330c1e" 17592186433144 "answer-field-b6bbf667-f0ec-5670-9c25-934e766d8f09" 17592186433145 "answer-field-c1c41282-b496-53fd-b601-ecca0a4b4a0c" 17592186433146 "answer-field-e09f4fd9-728c-5fad-860e-671199dd6464" 17592186433147 "answer-field-ec92482d-c477-5d24-9a25-7a7af7728403" 17592186433148 "question-93a